    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d577">

                <pb facs="0683" n="1517" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1517"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/652</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-07-30T18:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 30, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7.15 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>720. War Trade Board from Taylor:</p>
                <p>No. 1133. Replying your 915.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d577-f1"/> I advise you to
                    trade exports against tin and quinine and anything else you want. My last cable
                    expresses my feeling that a general agreement will be long delayed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d577-f1" n="1" xml:id="d577-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; see War Trade Board’s telegram No. 881, Department’s No. 289, July
                    18, <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg1515">p. 1515</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
